 "literal": "hard pebbles clear water having drunk stag its / sweet mate with turning around to frisk / itself came indeed rain-giving cool rainy season / without coming he who stays coming desiring / suffering aching to stay did you remain? thus asking",
 "translation": "The cool, rain-giving winter has arrived, as if to ask: 'Are you still waiting here, suffering and aching, longing for the return of him who stays away without coming back?'—even as the forest stag, having drunk the clear water flowing over hard pebbles, leaps and frisks in joy with its sweet mate.",
 "modern": "கடினமான பருக்கைக் கற்களின் மேல் ஓடும் தெளிந்த நீரைப் பருகிய ஆண் மான், தன் இனிய துணையோடு கூடித் துள்ளி விளையாட, மழை தரும் குளிர்ந்த கார்காலம் தானே வந்துவிட்டது. 'வராமல் பிரிந்து வாழும் தலைவனின் வருகையை விரும்பி, துன்புற்று மனவருத்தத்துடன் இன்னும் உயிரோடு இருக்கிறீர்களா?' என்று கேட்பது போல அது வந்துள்ளது.",
 "explanation": "This poem is set in the Mullai landscape, which represents the theme of patient waiting during the rainy season. The heroine speaks to her companion, expressing her deep grief at the arrival of the cool winter rains. She personifies the season, imagining that it has arrived specifically to mock her, asking if she is still alive and suffering while waiting for her lover. In contrast to her lonely state, she observes a forest stag that has drunk clear water from the pebbled stream and is happily playing with its mate. This natural imagery of animal union intensifies the heroine's sense of isolation and longing.",
